Z||000015||DRH||05-11-2000||05:43 PM||||Fierodave,<P>A thousand apologies... I did not mean to imply you were REALLY doing anything like that. It was meant as a joke... That's why the icon is the little winking guy. I thought everyone was aware that most (if not all) filter software will block sites for no good reason. Read Darklords comments...<P>I apologize for my own stupidity in not realizing that someone might not realize what I posted was a joke. I did not mean to insult you in any way. You are a very valuable member of this community and I hope you do not leave because of my not thinking.<P>David.<P><BR>PS: I really did get that message. You should be aware that if the filter software my company is using is blocking your address, most likely others will too. <BR>||||reg||11
Z||000016||Oreif||05-11-2000||05:51 PM||||Hey Fierodave, Relax. DHR's firewall has the settings to detect certain words that some geek put in. There are a few sites I cannot access at work because of the "key" words. Rodney's site is one of them. As an example of the geek factor, I could not enter a film site that let you view movie trailers because of the movie Malcolm X. Since the site had the X as a seperate and capitol "X" and it had the word "film" in the site title It wouldn't let me access it. It thought it was a porn site. Our firewall at work will tell you what it found for key words and if you need to access the site for work purposes (and it's not an adult site) they will add it to an authorized site list. By the way there is a porn star using "fiero" as a screen name. I have seen the video titles appear on Ebay when I look thru the fiero stuff.||||reg||4
